\title{The Weight-Quantisation Floor Is Not a Bit-Width Law\\[0.4em]
\large A geometric degradation ladder, a curvature ceiling, a selection/content
dissociation, and an exact accounting of the quantiser-quality confound}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{26 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
A widely repeated claim about compressed neural language models holds that weight
precision below roughly six bits per weight is undeployable: a \emph{floor}. We
report a seven-rung measurement on a $7$-billion-parameter transformer, evaluated
on a fixed held-out text slice, showing that excess perplexity over the
uncompressed reference is instead a smooth, strictly convex, single-parameter
geometric function of bit width across the entire range from $6.6$ down to $2.6$
bits per weight, with no cliff at any rung. Over \emph{all ten} ordered pairs of
measured rungs, excess perplexity multiplies by a factor in $[5/2,3]$ per bit
removed, and the lowest rung, $2.6$ bits per weight, costs only $+16.155\%$
relative perplexity. We then supply the theory. A second-order (curvature) model
predicts a degradation multiplier of \emph{exactly} $4$ per bit; the measured
ladder runs at $2.54$--$2.98$ per bit, strictly under this ceiling at every pair.
In the same model, improving quantiser accuracy by a factor $2^{j}$ is exactly
equivalent to adding $j$ bits, whence \emph{no bit width is intrinsically a
floor}. The contrast with the attention-cache axis, where cached \emph{keys}
degrade catastrophically between $8$ and $5$ bits, is a theorem: content
read-outs (probability-weighted averages) admit the sharp modulus of continuity
$\delta$ in the quantiser step, whereas selection read-outs ($\arg\max$) admit no
modulus of continuity whatsoever, and the number of broken decisions is bounded
by the number of positions with top-$1$ margin below $2\delta$. Composed
compressions obey a seminorm triangle inequality, giving the two-axis budget
$a+b+2\sqrt{ab}$ (under $3\%$ at the measured numbers), with exact additivity
under curvature-orthogonality ($1.956\%$ predicted). Finally we quantify the
quality-versus-scale confound: per-block scaling is worth exactly
$\log_2(R/\rms(r))$ bits, at most $\tfrac12\log_2 B$ (four bits at the standard
block size $B=256$), attained exactly under single-block outlier concentration,
and the observed $3.4$-bit floor shift fits inside that budget without invoking
model scale.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} weight quantisation, perplexity ladder, geometric
degradation law, Hessian curvature model, selection versus content, block
scaling, outlier concentration, seminorm composition.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The floor claim}

Post-training quantisation replaces a network's parameters with values drawn from
a coarse grid; the compression ratio is quoted in \emph{bits per weight} (bpw). A
persistent claim holds that there is a \emph{floor}: a bit width below which
quality collapses irrecoverably, commonly placed near six bits per weight.

The claim has empirical support of a specific kind. Take a small network and a
naive round-to-nearest quantiser using a \emph{single} scale factor per tensor.
Sweep the bit width. Quality is essentially unchanged down to six bits, degrades
sharply at five, and is destroyed at four. The curve visibly breaks rather than
bends, and it is natural to read the break as a property of the bit width.

\subsection{What this paper establishes}

We show the break is not a property of the bit width, in three senses.
\emph{Empirically}: at $14\times$ the model scale with calibration-aware block
quantisers, no break exists anywhere down to $2.6$ bpw, and the
excess-perplexity curve is strictly decreasing, strictly convex, and obeys a
single-parameter geometric law over its entire measured extent.
\emph{Structurally}: a per-bit multiplicative bound propagates geometrically over
any number of bits, and a geometric series has no pole, so ``no cliff between
adjacent rungs'' upgrades automatically to ``no floor at any finite bit width''.
\emph{Model-theoretically}: in the second-order model of quantisation damage,
quantiser accuracy $c$ and bit width $b$ appear only through the product
$c\,2^{-b}$, so naming a bit width as a floor without naming the quantiser is
ill-posed. We further explain why certain axes \emph{do} cliff, by proving a
dissociation between channels consumed by averaging and channels consumed by
ranking.

\subsection{Experimental setting}

All perplexity numbers come from one configuration: a $7$B-parameter transformer,
context length $2048$, eight CPU threads, and a fixed $250\,\mathrm{KB}$ held-out
text slice. The uncompressed ($16$-bit) reference perplexity is $6.9825$. One
cross-check: the $4.8$ bpw arm reproduced an earlier independent run's control
perplexity of $7.1093$ exactly, same model file and slice, days apart.

\section{The measured ladder}

\subsection{Data and definitions}

